{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-217488","model_call":23,"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"why\" \"everyone is watching\" \"spotlight effect\" (+ overestimate attention judging))"},"record":{"query":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"why\" \"everyone is watching\" \"spotlight effect\" (+ overestimate attention judging))"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_30_1526547997#7_3237842848","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q3758","rank":1,"snippet":"as repeated, meaningless sounds. 7. Reminiscence bump: a phenomenon in which older adults have an increased tendency to recollect memories which occurred from 16 to 25 years of age. 8. Dunning-Kruger Effect: a term to explain <cover>why ignorant people tend to boast about their confidence in spite of being wrong, and wiser people often have doubts about their knowledge. 9. Mean World Syndrome: a phenomenon in which violence related to mass media content makes viewers believe that the world is a dangerous place than it actually is. 10. Impostor phenomenon: a phenomenon in which an individual questions their accomplishments and faces anxiety of being exposed as a “fraud”. \" , \"segment\": \"Spotlight Effect: a phenomenon that makes people significantly overestimate how noticeable their embarrassing behaviors are to others. This effect occurs due to cognitive bias. Share Share on Pinterest Share on Facebook Share on Twitter Have you ever walked into a room and felt like everyone is watching </cover>and judging you? If you have felt that way, you have experienced the “spotlight effect.” The spotlight effect is a cognitive bias in which an individual thinks they are being continuously observed, noticed, and judged by others. "},"time":38},"model_calls":23,"document_judgments":19,"relevance_3":8,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
