{"actor":"server","event":"request","topic_id":"2024-217488","model_call":33,"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ hypervigilance \"being watched\" (+ trauma ptsd anxiety))"},"model_calls":33,"document_judgments":29,"relevance_3":15,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-217488","model_call":33,"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ hypervigilance \"being watched\" (+ trauma ptsd anxiety))"},"record":{"query":{"op":"query","query":"(^ hypervigilance \"being watched\" (+ trauma ptsd anxiety))"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_49_1601945736#4_3353439309","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q3763","rank":1,"snippet":"\" , \"segment\": \"Stress hormones such as cortisol and norepinephrine flood the body, which prepares to defend itself. During this time, our body automatically tenses up. The problem is that tension doesn’t always dissipate once the threat is removed or neutralized. In this case, the brain’s neural networks will maintain a state of <cover>hypervigilance, acting as if the threat remains and preparing the body accordingly. 2. Social withdrawal Research demonstrates that childhood trauma contributes to Social Anxiety Disorder or SAD. Parental abuse (e.g., denigrating, insulting, swearing, verbal aggression) and emotional neglect (i.e., not feeling cared for, loved, or nurtured) relate directly to the onset of SAD. Victims of childhood trauma who demonstrate social withdrawal often do so out of anxiety and fear. SAD patients report feeling “an intense, persistent fear of being watched </cover>and judged by others,” and may isolate themselves to prevent this feeling. 3.\" , \"start_char\": 1778.000000 , \"title\": \"5 Adult Behaviors of Someone That Experienced Trauma As A Child\" , \"url\": \"https://www.powerofpositivity.com/5-adult-behaviors-someone-experienced-trauma-child/"},"time":50},"model_calls":33,"document_judgments":29,"relevance_3":15,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
